# {{ full_project_name }}

Versioned data products, brought into a locked-down server.

## How it works

`enclave_manifest.yaml` lists the data products approved for this enclave,
each pinned to a producer commit. On a networked machine, `mintd enclave
package` imports exactly those versions and bundles the ones not yet sent into
one zip. Inside the enclave, you unzip it in this folder and check every file
with the system's own MD5 tool. Nothing inside needs Python or mintd.

## 1. Approve a product (networked machine)

Add a row to `approved_products` in `enclave_manifest.yaml`:

```yaml
approved_products:
  - repo: my-cleaned-survey                       # as `mintd data list` shows it
    pin: 0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef01234567  # a full producer commit
    # source_path: data/final/survey.parquet      # one output; omit for the primary
    # all: true                                   # or every output at that pin
```

To move to a newer version, change `pin`. `mintd check --upgrades` lists the
imports whose producer has moved on.

## 2. Package (networked machine)

```bash
mintd enclave package
# Creates transfers/transfer-YYYY-MM-DD-NNNNNN.zip
```

Each approved version is imported into `data/imports/` as `mintd data import`
would, and every file's MD5 is checked against its import as it goes into the
zip. Bundles are **incremental**: versions already recorded in `transferred`
are skipped, and when nothing is new it says so and exits 0. A bundle counts
as sent when it is built; if one is lost in transit, re-ship it with
`mintd enclave package --resend`.

## 3. Land it (enclave)

Copy the zip into this folder, then run the two commands for your system from
here. On Linux:

```bash
unzip -n transfer-YYYY-MM-DD-NNNNNN.zip
md5sum -c transfers/transfer-YYYY-MM-DD-NNNNNN/MD5SUMS
```

`unzip -n` never overwrites a file already in place. The check prints one line
per file, and each should end in `OK`. The `README.md` the zip unzips under
`transfers/<transfer id>/` gives the same two steps for macOS and Windows.

## Data layout

```
data/
└── <product>/
    └── <commit date>_<first 7 characters of the pin>/
        └── <the producer's path, e.g. data/final/>
transfers/
└── <transfer id>/      MD5SUMS, manifest.json and README.md for one bundle
```

A newer version lands in its own folder beside the older one. Never edit a
version folder by hand. Each bundle adds one folder under `transfers/`, the
record of what landed.

## Security notes

- Every version is pinned by producer commit and every file by MD5, from the
  import pointer to the zip to the check inside.
- `transferred` in `enclave_manifest.yaml` is append-only: mintd only adds lines
  to the end of the file.
- Transfer zips and `data/` are git-ignored, so restricted data cannot be
  committed by accident; `transfers/<transfer id>/` records are tracked.

